WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.520
The gritty world of "The Batman" is filled with&nbsp;
intrigue and corruption. But as the credits roll,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.360
there is a lot left unresolved. Keep&nbsp;
watching to learn the biggest unanswered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.360 --> 00:00:11.600
questions from "The Batman."

00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.080
Perhaps the biggest tease for Batman's future&nbsp;
clash with villainy is the mysterious character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.320
sitting in the cell right next to the Riddler&nbsp;
at the end of the film. He offers the Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:25.120
friendship or an alliance in an effort to stage&nbsp;
a "comeback" story for the people of Gotham.&nbsp;

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.600
At first glance, it seems like the figure might&nbsp;
be some kind of revamped take on Two-Face.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:34.640
His hair is unkempt and it appears that his face&nbsp;
may be disfigured, at least on the one side that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:38.960
the audience is allowed to see. Furthermore,&nbsp;
Batman fans will recognize that Carmine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:44.960
Falcone's operations were busted wide open by law&nbsp;
enforcement prior to the events of the film. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:50.160
Jeph Loeb's "The Long Halloween," it's Falcone's&nbsp;
legal woes and his rivalry with Sal Maroni that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:55.280
result in Harvey Dent being splashed with acid&nbsp;
by a mobster during his testimony in court.&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.560
This could have already occurred prior to "The&nbsp;
Batman," especially since Gil Colson is the D.A.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:04.800
and there is no mention of Harvey Dent.&nbsp;
However, that cackle isn't necessarily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:09.280
something Two-Face is known for. In fact,&nbsp;
it's far more reminiscent of the Joker.&nbsp;

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:14.080
"Just an old friend, who's&nbsp;
dying to do me a favor.”&nbsp;

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:19.680
[cackling]

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:23.840
The Riddler is very thorough when he goes&nbsp;
combing through the history books of Gotham City&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:28.800
in an attempt to eliminate every bit of corruption&nbsp;
he can find. The Riddler's mission becomes clear:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:33.280
kill a corrupt official, then destroy their&nbsp;
reputation by outing their dirty deeds on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.880
nightly news. During his vigilante crusade, he&nbsp;
targets the Wayne family. Among his newspaper&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:43.120
clippings is one that highlights a seeming&nbsp;
partnership between Thomas Wayne and crime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:48.640
boss Carmine Falcone. And in that stack, one&nbsp;
headline can be seen referencing Edward Elliot.&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:53.440
The Riddler clearly has his eye on the Wayne and&nbsp;
Arkham families who helped establish Gotham City.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:58.080
However, Edward Elliot was also a wealthy&nbsp;
aristocrat who played a fundamental role in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.160
modernizing the city. In the comics,&nbsp;
he's an ancestor of Thomas Elliot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:07.200
who was Bruce Wayne's friend when they were kids.
He later turned to a life of crime, adopting the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:13.360
moniker of Hush and using his talents and medical&nbsp;
expertise to wage war against Bruce. Underneath it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:18.080
all, Thomas was envious of Bruce's life despite&nbsp;
the tragedy that he suffered in Crime Alley as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.080 --> 00:02:23.360
a young boy. This villain's nefarious deeds are&nbsp;
ultimately very personal for Batman as a result.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:28.720
Now that we know the Elliot family exists within&nbsp;
the Gotham City of "The Batman," there's always a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.720 --> 00:02:31.520
chance that Hush could take center stage.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.560
The Riddler highlighted two of&nbsp;
Gotham's most elite families:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.440
the Waynes and the Arkhams. During the Riddler's&nbsp;
crusade against the corrupt powers in Gotham,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.000
he discusses Thomas Wayne's culpability for&nbsp;
working with the mob and associating with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.920
the likes of Carmine Falcone. Even though&nbsp;
the Riddler brings up the two families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.400
he really only focuses on the Waynes and their&nbsp;
closeted collaboration with organized crime.&nbsp;

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.800
"You're a part of this too."
"How am I a part of this?"&nbsp;

00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:04.240
"You'll see."
The Arkham family is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.240 --> 00:03:09.040
never discussed in great detail. Is the Arkham&nbsp;
family still active in Gotham? Or is the famous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:13.760
holding asylum that bears their family name the&nbsp;
only real claim to fame? Clearly, the Riddler&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:18.560
didn't attack anyone within the Arkham family.&nbsp;
That could be because their lineage is no more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.560 --> 00:03:23.360
or because the living Arkham family members are&nbsp;
squeaky clean. Perhaps Batman's next foe will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:26.560
teach us more about what happened to the Arkhams.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.520
The Riddler made his capture rather easy. Clearly,&nbsp;
he had an endgame in mind that the Gotham police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.520 --> 00:03:36.080
department wasn't privy to. And neither was&nbsp;
Batman, despite the Riddler's attempts to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:40.240
enlighten him. When we see the Riddler sitting&nbsp;
on the opposite side of hardened glass talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:45.440
to Batman about his plan, viewers can see clearly&nbsp;
that he has an "Arkham State Hospital" suit on.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:49.760
Why is the facility not referred to as&nbsp;
Arkham Asylum? Will the State Hospital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.760 --> 00:03:53.440
undergo changes after a new surge in&nbsp;
high-profile villains make their way into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:58.640
police custody after being captured by Batman?
In the film "Joker," Joaquin Phoenix's version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.440
the character is seen standing outside of Arkham&nbsp;
State Hospital as well. Perhaps this is also the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:06.560
version of Arkham we see in "The Batman."

00:04:06.560 --> 00:04:10.000
Alfred Pennyworth is the closest&nbsp;
Bruce Wayne has to a family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:14.000
In most iterations of the character, he&nbsp;
is a faithful butler who served the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.000 --> 00:04:18.320
for many years. He helped care for Bruce&nbsp;
following the tragic death of his parents.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:23.360
In "The Batman," Alfred receives the wrath of&nbsp;
the Riddler by mistake as a mail bomb detonates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:27.840
The lethal package was actually meant for&nbsp;
Bruce. Fortunately, Alfred manages to survive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.480 --> 00:04:33.120
although he's hospitalized in critical condition.
Later, Alfred reveals that he was the one who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:37.360
taught Bruce to fight. As evidenced during&nbsp;
the fight sequences seen in "The Batman,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:41.520
those lessons must have included a whole&nbsp;
lot more than simply how to throw a punch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:46.240
If Batman's combat prowess is any&nbsp;
indication, Alfred is a formidable fighter.&nbsp;

00:04:46.240 --> 00:04:50.320
Bruce's confidant also tries to help him&nbsp;
come to terms with feelings of guilt over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.320 --> 00:04:54.560
his parents' deaths. He reminds the crime&nbsp;
fighter that he was a young boy at the time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:58.800
Alfred explains that he was the one responsible&nbsp;
for protecting them. Does that mean Alfred&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:03.280
acts more like a bodyguard than a butler?
In the Earth One comic book storyline, Alfred is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.800
a battle-hardened fighter who trained Bruce in all&nbsp;
that he knows. As a former member of the S.A.S.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:13.520
Alfred likely knew a thing or two about combat&nbsp;
and survival. Perhaps the Alfred we see in "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.480
Batman" shares a similar backstory.
&nbsp;

00:05:16.480 --> 00:05:21.760
Make no mistake, Batman can take a beating. That's&nbsp;
in no small part thanks to his apparently stellar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.760 --> 00:05:26.640
armor, although we don't really know exactly why&nbsp;
Batman's suit is so good at repelling damage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:30.880
There's obviously a heavy degree of Kevlar&nbsp;
involved. Batman takes several bullets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:36.000
throughout the film, including a shotgun blast&nbsp;
directly to the chest, and he somehow manages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:40.880
to walk away every time. That raises the&nbsp;
question, what is Batman's armor made of?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:46.320
Kevlar can only do so much. It's pretty clear that&nbsp;
Bruce has some top-of-the-line gear. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:51.440
he wears a pair of contacts that work as spy&nbsp;
cameras capable of recording everything he sees.&nbsp;

00:05:51.440 --> 00:05:55.680
At one point in the film, he has to escape the&nbsp;
police from inside the precinct. He and Jim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.680 --> 00:06:00.160
Gordon stage a faux assault and Batman flees to&nbsp;
the top of the building, where he leaps from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.160 --> 00:06:06.000
roof to glide safely away from danger. He doesn't&nbsp;
exactly land gracefully, however. His parachute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.600
catches a bridge and slams his body into a vehicle&nbsp;
and the concrete. It's a pretty nasty spill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.600 --> 00:06:16.400
and he limps away in a significant amount of pain.&nbsp;
However, the whole engagement makes us wonder what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:21.440
type of armor could possibly protect Batman from&nbsp;
colliding at a high speed with concrete and metal.&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:23.680
Fans know well that Batman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:30.080
prepares for just about any probable scenario.
"Where does he get those wonderful toys?"&nbsp;

00:06:30.080 --> 00:06:34.080
He often has access to a variety of tools&nbsp;
or gadgets that he might need to rely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.080 --> 00:06:38.560
on in a pinch in order to narrowly avert&nbsp;
catastrophe. In the final moments of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:42.640
Batman is fighting several of the Riddler's&nbsp;
followers, who are all armed to the teeth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.640 --> 00:06:46.480
One particular baddie knocks the wind out&nbsp;
of the Dark Knight with a rather close-range&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.480 --> 00:06:51.680
shotgun blast. Batman is visibly injured&nbsp;
and likely suffering from fractured ribs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:56.160
Selina Kyle enters the fray to save Batman&nbsp;
but quickly finds herself taking heat from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.160 --> 00:07:00.880
another goon. In an effort to save Selina,&nbsp;
Batman pulls out a strange green fluid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.880 --> 00:07:06.000
and injects himself with it, after which he&nbsp;
gets up and pummels his foe in a fit of rage.&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.160
Now, what do we know about a green fluid that&nbsp;
bolsters physical performance dramatically and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.160 --> 00:07:14.000
might even cause someone to lose control&nbsp;
of their anger? Bane might know the answer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:18.480
to this question. Because, in the comics, Venom&nbsp;
is the name of a drug the back-breaking villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.480 --> 00:07:23.760
uses in an attempt to overpower Batman. The&nbsp;
question "The Batman" viewers are left asking is:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.760 --> 00:07:27.840
Was that vial filled with Venom? If&nbsp;
so, why would Batman be carrying it?&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:30.800
At the start of "The Batman," the Penguin isn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:34.960
quite the high-profile gangster he's known for&nbsp;
being throughout his rich history in the comics.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.960 --> 00:07:39.680
When we first see him, he's still under Carmine&nbsp;
Falcone's thumb. Of course, this changes when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:44.880
Riddler murders Falcone, creating a power vacuum&nbsp;
in Gotham's organized crime structure that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:49.120
Penguin is poised to take full advantage of.
At the end of the film, we see a shot of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.120 --> 00:07:53.520
Penguin contemplatively looking out at Gotham.&nbsp;
It's clear that he's about to ascend the ranks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:58.160
of his criminal empire. So what does the future&nbsp;
hold for the Penguin after the credits roll?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:02.720
Does this position him to be a prime target for&nbsp;
Batman in an upcoming sequel? At some point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:07.200
we'll be treated to a look at the Penguin prior&nbsp;
to the events of "The Batman" with his own spinoff&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:12.320
series on HBO Max. Perhaps the show will shed some&nbsp;
light on the character's plans for the future.&nbsp;

00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:15.520
Batman's partnership with the GCPD,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:21.120
and more specifically Jim Gordon, is one of the&nbsp;
most famous aspects of the Dark Knight's legacy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.120 --> 00:08:25.280
In times of trouble, Police Commissioner&nbsp;
Jim Gordon often flips on the Batsignal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:29.840
summoning the Caped Crusader. But in "The&nbsp;
Batman," Jim Gordon is still a lieutenant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:35.040
and hasn't yet risen to the position of police&nbsp;
commissioner. That role belongs to Pete Savage.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:39.680
Unfortunately for the commissioner, however, his&nbsp;
dirty deeds are brought to light by the Riddler.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:45.120
Rather quickly, the villain releases a snuff film&nbsp;
of sorts putting Savage's demise on full display.&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.040
With murder and mayhem on the agenda for&nbsp;
Gotham, there isn't any time to discuss&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:53.200
replacements for the corrupt commissioner&nbsp;
during the events of "The Batman." However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:57.280
when the dust settles, perhaps Gordon will&nbsp;
be promoted to the job he's most famous for.&nbsp;

00:08:57.280 --> 00:09:01.600
It's not a foregone conclusion, though. There are&nbsp;
those in the police force who don't condone Jim's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:07.040
work with Batman. Still, it seems like a safe bet&nbsp;
that we'll see Gordon climb the ranks in a sequel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.040 --> 00:09:10.400
opening more doors for the blossoming&nbsp;
partnership between the Dark Knight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.400 --> 00:09:13.040
and Gotham's police commissioner.

00:09:13.040 --> 00:09:17.440
Coming from an affluent family, Bruce Wayne has&nbsp;
always enjoyed the luxuries of Wayne Manor. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.440 --> 00:09:22.240
uses his family's private estate as a bunker-like&nbsp;
home base for his personal, professional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.240 --> 00:09:27.520
and crime fighting operations. However, the&nbsp;
mansion is curiously absent from "The Batman."&nbsp;

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.760
Instead, Bruce seems to live in a skyscraper&nbsp;
looking down upon the city of Gotham.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.760 --> 00:09:36.960
His "Batcave" appears to be in an abandoned subway&nbsp;
tunnel. This raises the question of whether Wayne&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:42.240
Manor exists in Matt Reeves' version of the Batman&nbsp;
mythos. At one point, Riddler voices his disdain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:46.720
for Bruce Wayne as a wealthy orphan who had&nbsp;
everything. He claims Bruce "looked down" upon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.720 --> 00:09:51.360
the less fortunate, suggesting that he might have&nbsp;
always lived in the skyscraper in Gotham City.&nbsp;

00:09:52.160 --> 00:09:55.920
"Your family has a history of philanthropy, but&nbsp;
as far as I can tell, you're not doing anything."&nbsp;

00:09:56.640 --> 00:10:01.040
However, if Wayne Manor and the Batcave do&nbsp;
exist, perhaps they're undergoing renovations&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.040 --> 00:10:05.280
much like in "The Dark Knight" following Ra's&nbsp;
Al Ghul's assault on the Wayne home. There's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:09.680
also potential for Bruce to build a new home&nbsp;
that's strategically placed over a cave he can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:12.160
utilize for his operations.

00:10:12.160 --> 00:10:16.560
The answer to this question varies depending&nbsp;
on which version of the Batman story you read,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:20.720
watch, or hear. In some versions, Thomas and&nbsp;
Martha Wayne were simply in the wrong place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:25.280
at the wrong time. The man named Joe Chill&nbsp;
is often referenced in Batman lore as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.280 --> 00:10:29.760
thug who gunned down Bruce's parents. Other&nbsp;
versions of the story indicate that the mob&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:34.080
played a role in Thomas and Martha's deaths.
In "The Batman," Bruce confronts Carmine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:39.760
Falcone over the deaths of his parents. He learns&nbsp;
a hard truth. Carmine claims Thomas Wayne came to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:44.640
Falcone asking him for his help in dealing with&nbsp;
a reporter spreading harmful information about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.640 --> 00:10:50.320
Martha. Falcone, in a calculated move, had the&nbsp;
reporter killed. But as he goes on to tell Bruce,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:54.560
he's also heard that his rival, Sal&nbsp;
Maroni, had Bruce's parents murdered.&nbsp;

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:58.400
Bruce then approaches Alfred with this&nbsp;
knowledge, and Alfred claims that Thomas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.400 --> 00:11:02.240
didn't realize Falcone would help him&nbsp;
by actually having the reporter killed.&nbsp;

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:07.360
"You lied to me, Alfred."
Burdened with guilt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:11.520
he resolved to go to the authorities but&nbsp;
was killed that night he planned to do it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.520 --> 00:11:15.120
This leads Bruce to believe that it&nbsp;
was Falcone who killed his parents.&nbsp;

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:19.920
For his part, Alfred admits that he hoped to&nbsp;
find a smoking gun identifying the guilty party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.920 --> 00:11:25.520
but was never able to do so. He can't confirm&nbsp;
whether Falcone did the deed. With Falcone dead&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.520 --> 00:11:30.080
at the end of "The Batman," we won't hear more&nbsp;
from him about it. If another player was involved,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:34.880
it'll likely play a pivotal role in Bruce's future&nbsp;
conflicts with the evil that plagues Gotham.&nbsp;

